7592186414415 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186414304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414415 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186414335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414415 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186414352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414415 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414415 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414415 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186414304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414415 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ff14ff76-eafd-553a-b96d-1e02876d3683" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414416 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414416 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414416 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414416 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181633" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414416 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414416 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nC=[-1,1],\\,D={n<5∣\\,n\\in N}\n$$\n\nComplete the following reasoning about the sets $C$ and $D$ (given above):\n\nSince $C$ is {{field-1}} and $D$ is {{field-2}}, we have that {{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414416 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "003c5be3-9b52-54de-b5e9-0ebd26752121" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414416 #edb/ref 1074 "The cardinalities of two sets cannot be equal if one set is finite and the other is infinite.\n\nWith that in mind, notice that \n\n- $C$ is infinite (since it's an interval of real numbers), while\n\n- $D={1,2,3,4}$ is finite with $|D|=4,$ .\n\nTherefore, $|C|\\ne |D|.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414417 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414417 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181624" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414417 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414417 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nX={a,b,2,4},\\,Y={n\\in N∣\\,1\\le n<5}\n$$\n\nConsider the sets $X$ and $Y$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $|X|=4$\n- $|Y|=5$\n- $|X|=|Y|$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414417 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1261accc-9253-5b2b-9450-d1cf9dd3a558" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414417 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that finite sets have the same cardinality if and only if they have the same number of elements.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $X$ is a finite set and $|X|=4.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that $Y={1,2,3,4}$ is a finite set and $|Y|=4.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $X$ and $Y$ are finite sets and  $|X|=|Y|=4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414418 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414418 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414418 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414418 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181681" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414418 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414418 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA={2,0,-2,-4,…},\\,B=N\n$$\n\nComplete the following reasoning about the sets $A$ and $B$ (shown above):\n\nSince the function defined for each $n\\in N$ as\n\n$$\nf:n\\to {{field-1}}\n$$\n\nis a {{field-2}} of $B$ onto $A,$ we have that {{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414418 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1e8f5aa7-7ccd-5ed3-b543-c8ead40f433d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414418 #edb/ref 1074 "Two sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B.$\n\nNotice that the elements of $A$ form an (infinite) arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=-2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =-2 \\\\  & =0-2 \\\\  & =-2-0 \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =2+(n-1)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =2-2n+2 \\\\  & =-2n+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as \n\n$$\nA={-2n+4∣\\,n\\in N}.\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $f:B\\to A$ that maps the underlying sets as follows: \n\n| $B$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $⋯$ | $n$ | $⋯$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $A$ | $2$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-4$ | $⋯$ | $-2n+4$ | $⋯$ |\n\nIn general, for each $n\\in B,$ we have $f(n)=-2n+4\\in A.$\n\nNotice that $f$ is described in such a way that it is bijective. Indeed, it's\n\n- injective since $f(n)\\ne f(m)$ whenever $n\\ne m,$ and\n\n- surjective since each element in $A$ (the second row) has a pre-image in $B$ (the first row).\n\nTherefore, $|A|=|B|,$ i.e., the sets have the same cardinality." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414419 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414419 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414419 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414419 #edb/ref 1069 "q-216534" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414419 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414419 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=N,\\,B={3,1,-1,-3,…}\n$$\n\nComplete the following reasoning about the sets $A$ and $B$ above:\n\nSince the function defined for each $n\\in N$ as\n\n$$\nf:n\\to {{field-1}}\n$$\n\nis a {{field-2}} of $A$ onto $B,$ we have that {{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414419 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2a225633-5b65-55d6-85ca-1c31e57a48fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186414419 #edb/ref 1074 "Two sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B.$\n\nNotice that the elements of $B$ form an (infinite) arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=-2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =-2 \\\\  & =1-3 \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted ${b}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =3+(n-1)(-2) \\\\  & =3-2n+2 \\\\  & =5-2n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTher